WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.130
This chapter covers how equipment is made safe to reuse.

2
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:16.290
We start with the difference between sterilization and disinfection, and the Spaulding categories that decide which one a device needs.

3
00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:25.219
Then four methods the study guide names: pasteurization, the autoclave, isopropyl alcohol and chlorhexidine.

4
00:00:25.219 --> 00:00:32.825
We finish with glutaraldehyde, OPA, bleach, ethylene oxide and hydrogen peroxide gas plasma.

5
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:42.144
Hess defines sterilization as the complete destruction of all microorganisms, including spores.

6
00:00:42.144 --> 00:00:51.055
Disinfection kills most organisms but not all, and spores, mycobacteria and viruses are the most resistant.

7
00:00:51.055 --> 00:00:56.884
Cleaning always comes first, because any residue interferes with both.

8
00:00:56.884 --> 00:01:06.128
The key point from the deck: high-level disinfection is not sterilization, because bacterial spores may remain.

9
00:01:06.128 --> 00:01:22.033
That matters because most respiratory therapy equipment touches mucous membranes, so a reusable device such as a bronchoscope gets sterilization or high-level disinfection, never just a wipe.

10
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:29.690
The Spaulding system processes equipment by where it goes in the patient.

11
00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:39.032
Critical items enter sterile tissue or the bloodstream, so they must be sterilized, with no viable organisms.

12
00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:44.946
Examples are bronchoscope biopsy forceps, and Hess adds a chest tube.

13
00:01:44.946 --> 00:01:53.260
Semicritical items touch mucous membranes, so they need high-level disinfection or sterilization.

14
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:59.860
A bronchoscope is semicritical, and so is most respiratory therapy equipment.

15
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:12.716
Noncritical items touch only intact skin, so they need cleaning or low-level disinfection: stethoscopes, blood pressure cuffs and ventilator surfaces.

16
00:02:12.716 --> 00:02:23.258
Noncritical surfaces such as tables and floors are cleaned or disinfected regularly, after spills, and when visibly soiled.

17
00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:26.463
Pause and sort these.

18
00:02:26.463 --> 00:02:33.390
A reusable bronchoscope, the biopsy forceps passed through it, and a stethoscope.

19
00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:39.292
Which Spaulding category is each, and what processing does each need?

20
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:46.200
[6-second practice pause]

21
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:56.462
The bronchoscope touches mucous membranes, so it is semicritical and needs high-level disinfection or sterilization.

22
00:02:56.462 --> 00:03:03.615
The biopsy forceps enter sterile tissue, so they are critical and must be sterilized.

23
00:03:03.615 --> 00:03:13.125
The stethoscope touches only intact skin, so it is noncritical, and cleaning or low-level disinfection is enough.

24
00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:17.799
Pasteurization is hot water disinfection.

25
00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:24.249
Hess gives about seventy degrees Celsius for thirty minutes, followed by a dryer.

26
00:03:24.249 --> 00:03:33.647
It does not sterilize; the deck uses the milk and juice example to show that some non-pathogenic bacteria may survive.

27
00:03:33.647 --> 00:03:40.177
It suits heat-sensitive items, and the deck groups it with glutaraldehyde and OPA.

28
00:03:40.177 --> 00:03:46.230
The autoclave uses saturated steam under pressure, which denatures proteins.

29
00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:58.414
Hess gives one hundred twenty one degrees for thirty minutes, or one hundred thirty two degrees for four minutes, both at fifteen pounds per square inch.

30
00:03:58.414 --> 00:04:01.520
It does sterilize, and it kills spores.

31
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:10.758
It is used for heat-resistant equipment that has been cleaned and wrapped first, and it is fast, cheap and nontoxic.

32
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:17.248
Isopropyl alcohol is a chemical that denatures proteins.

33
00:04:17.248 --> 00:04:24.960
It does not sterilize, it does not kill spores, and it may damage rubber and plastic.

34
00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:33.760
It is used on skin before injections or blood draws, on vial tops, thermometers and stethoscopes.

35
00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:41.563
Chlorhexidine is an antiseptic, for skin prep and oral care, not an equipment process.

36
00:04:41.563 --> 00:04:49.910
The deck's distinction: an antiseptic is used on living tissue, a disinfectant on equipment.

37
00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:58.620
Chlorhexidine scrubs the site before an arterial line and preps the skin before a thoracentesis.

38
00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:08.963
For VAP prevention, the RCP 203 chapter nine bundle lists daily oral care with chlorhexidine, and so does RCP 105.

39
00:05:08.963 --> 00:05:19.942
Hess says it is no longer recommended and may prove harmful in some patients, but for this exam, use the deck's VAP list.

40
00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:32.076
Glutaraldehyde, Cidex, gives high-level disinfection in twenty minutes at room temperature, but sterilization takes a full ten hours.

41
00:05:32.076 --> 00:05:40.129
The deck notes that times vary by product, so follow the manufacturer, and rinse with sterile water.

42
00:05:40.129 --> 00:05:52.610
Hess says it irritates the nose and airways, causing nosebleeds, rhinitis and asthma, and gives six to ten hours for sterilization, but use the deck's ten.

43
00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:58.328
OPA, ortho-phthalaldehyde, sterilizes or gives high-level disinfection.

44
00:05:58.328 --> 00:06:05.092
Hess gives a twelve minute exposure, and says it does not irritate the eyes or nose.

45
00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:15.786
For Clostridioides difficile, which forms spores, the deck says use an EPA-registered sporicidal disinfectant.

46
00:06:15.786 --> 00:06:25.241
For blood spills, if bleach is used, follow the product instructions and the facility protocol for the dilution.

47
00:06:25.241 --> 00:06:34.443
Only Hess gives a number: a one to ten dilution of five point two five percent bleach for blood spills and C.

48
00:06:34.443 --> 00:06:35.371
diff rooms.

49
00:06:35.371 --> 00:06:40.774
Never mix bleach with an acid, because it releases chlorine gas.

50
00:06:40.774 --> 00:06:48.287
Ethylene oxide is a low-temperature gas sterilizer for heat and moisture sensitive items.

51
00:06:48.287 --> 00:06:56.898
It is toxic, flammable and explosive, and items need eight to twenty four hours of aeration afterward.

52
00:06:56.898 --> 00:07:06.775
Hydrogen peroxide gas plasma is Hess's preferred sterilizer for items that cannot take high temperature and humidity.

53
00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:08.994
One more.

54
00:07:08.994 --> 00:07:22.750
Of these, which actually sterilize, killing spores: pasteurization, the autoclave, isopropyl alcohol, glutaraldehyde for twenty minutes, and ethylene oxide?

55
00:07:23.667 --> 00:07:29.667
[6-second practice pause]

56
00:07:30.167 --> 00:07:33.918
The autoclave and ethylene oxide sterilize.

57
00:07:33.918 --> 00:07:39.065
Pasteurization does not, because some bacteria may survive.

58
00:07:39.065 --> 00:07:41.595
Alcohol does not kill spores.

59
00:07:41.595 --> 00:07:51.367
And twenty minutes of glutaraldehyde gives only high-level disinfection; it takes a full ten hours to sterilize.

60
00:07:52.767 --> 00:07:55.330
Here is the chapter in brief.

61
00:07:55.330 --> 00:08:04.788
Sterilization kills everything, including spores; disinfection kills most; and cleaning always comes first.

62
00:08:04.788 --> 00:08:19.726
Critical items are sterilized, semicritical items, such as a bronchoscope and most respiratory equipment, get high-level disinfection, and noncritical items are cleaned.

63
00:08:19.726 --> 00:08:25.384
Pasteurization and alcohol do not sterilize; the autoclave does.

64
00:08:25.384 --> 00:08:30.422
Chlorhexidine is an antiseptic for tissue, not equipment.

65
00:08:30.422 --> 00:08:36.786
Glutaraldehyde disinfects in twenty minutes and sterilizes in ten hours.

66
00:08:36.786 --> 00:08:47.217
Ethylene oxide sterilizes but is toxic and needs aeration, and gas plasma is the preferred low-temperature sterilizer.
